\paragraph{Embedding gradient analysis.}
Table~\ref{tab:three_way_emb} isolates embedding \emph{trainability} as the necessary condition for CIVO by comparing four gradient conditions under otherwise identical configurations ($H{=}8$, subsample ON, $\dt{=}2$ imagination).

% tab:three_way_emb moved to main text (Section 3.4)

\paragraph{Controlled three-way causal ablation.}
Experiments 067--069 provide a minimal causal ablation on Walker Walk (seed${=}1$), varying \emph{only} the $\dt$-embedding treatment while holding all other hyperparameters fixed ($H{=}8$, subsample ON, $\dt{=}2$ imagination). Table~\ref{tab:causal_ablation} shows that trained $\dt$-embedding is necessary and sufficient for CIVO: freezing the embedding (gradient flow blocked, embedding weights still present) eliminates CIVO as completely as removing the embedding ($d{=}0$), ruling out the alternative hypothesis that embedding \emph{presence} alone causes CIVO.

\paragraph{Cross-environment frozen verification.}
Experiments 084--087 extend the frozen-embedding control to a $2 \times 2$ design (Walker $+$ Cheetah, 2 seeds each). All four runs confirm CIVO absence: exp\_084 (Walker, seed~0, NVE peak $0.53\times$, eval 821, 89\% baseline), exp\_085 (Walker, seed~1, NVE peak $0.31\times$), exp\_086 (Cheetah, seed~0, NVE $< 0.4\times$), and exp\_087 (Cheetah, seed~1, NVE $< 0.4\times$). Combined with all prior seeds, trained $\dt$-embedding triggers CIVO in 5/7 multi-joint locomotion runs versus 0/16 frozen or removed controls (Fisher exact $p < 0.001$); embedding \emph{training} is a necessary condition for CIVO across environments and seeds.

